{[%evp 0,144,26,34,86,77,83,44,73,63,75,-9,13,-48,23,-50,15,-5,-5,-5,32,-4,13,-17,32,26,26,-44,35,41,41,30,53,56,79,79,91,102,115,106,168,140,116,95,95,-148,196,191,164,119,119,225,225,217,217,217,217,345,236,236,271,55,32,-49,-57,-76,-76,-59,-20,-62,-62,-129,-153,-149,-46,-40,0,-11,0,-136,-83,-104,61,2,-51,-80,-40,-47,-136,-69,-69,-66,-66,-66,-66,-66,-65,-59,-59,-61,-61,-59,-59,-59,-59,-59,-59,-70,-59,-73,-84,-85,-59,-76,-59,-59,-56,-56,-59,-58,-59,-60,-59,-66,-59,-59,-59,-59,-65,-65,-65,-65,-65,-83,-85,-187,-85,-87,-87,-98,-98,-105,-163,-188,-134,-320,-353]} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 g6 4. O-O Bg7 5. c3 Nf6 6. Re1 O-O 7. h3 e5 8. d4 (8. d3 {is the main move}) 8... cxd4 9. cxd4 Nxd4 10. Nxd4 exd4 (10... Qb6 $5) 11. e5 $2 {The mistake that is being done quite often!} (11. Qxd4 {is the best continuation}) 11... Ne8 ({Black can also go for} 11... Nd5 $144 12. Qxd4 Qa5 $1 13. Rd1 Nc7 14. Bxd7 $4 (14. Bc4 Qxe5 $15) 14... Rd8 15. Qa4 Rxd7 $1 16. Qxa5 Rxd1+ 17. Kh2 Rxc1 $19 {0-1 (62) Rocha, S (2369) -Bocharov,D (2524) Cappelle la Grande 2003}) 12. Qxd4 d6 13. Nc3 Be6 $2 (13... Nc7 $1 {and Black wins the e5-pawn.}) 14. Bxe8 $1 Rxe8 15. Qxd6 $14 {now White's position is better} Qa5 16. Bg5 $1 {covering the d8-square and preparing Bf6 trading the dark-squared Bishops.} h6 17. Bf6 Bxf6 18. exf6 Rad8 19. Qf4 {Now, White has a extra pawn and an attack on Black's King} Kh7 $8 20. Re5 Qb6 21. Rb5 Qc6 22. Re1 $2 {what only doesn't happen in a blitz game?} Bxa2 $2 ({both sides overlooked the possibility of} 22... Bxh3 $1 {after what White barely stays in the game with} 23. Re4 $8 Rxe4 24. Qxe4 Qxe4 25. Nxe4 Bf5 $17) 23. Re7 b6 24. Nxa2 (24. b3 {was perhaps easier} Rf8 (24... Qxc3 25. Rxf7+ Kg8 26. Rg7+ Kf8 27. Qxh6 $18) 25. Rc7 $1 Qe6 26. Nxa2 $18) 24... Qxb5 25. Rxf7+ {now position seems to be crushing, but is it so elementary?} Kg8 26. Rg7+ Kf8 27. Qxh6 Qh5 $8 28. Rg8+ Kf7 29. Qg7+ Ke6 {[#] How to win the game?} 30. f7 {looks like winning the game on the spot...} (30. Nc3 $1 {would be the cleanest!} Rxg8 31. Qe7+ Kf5 32. g4+ $18) ({or} 30. Rxe8+ Rxe8 31. Nc3 Qh8 32. Qxg6 $18) {...But!} 30... Rf8 $3 {Came as a shocker for Idani! It turns out that Black stays in the game thanks to tactics!} 31. Nc3 $2 {I assume White was simply tilted after Black's last move and lost the control over he situation} (31. Rxf8 Qd1+ 32. Kh2 Qd6+ 33. g3 Rxf8 34. Nc3) 31... Rxf7 32. Qxg6+ Qxg6 33. Rxg6+ Ke7 {now the endgame is difficult for White} 34. h4 $8 Rd2 35. Ne4 Rxb2 36. h5 Rb5 37. g4 (37. h6 $142) 37... Re5 38. Nd6 Rf6 39. f4 Rxf4 40. h6 (40. Nf5+ Rexf5 41. gxf5 Rxf5 42. h6) 40... Rxg4+ 41. Rxg4 Kxd6 42. Rh4 (42. Rg6+ Kc5 43. Kf2 {would have been easier way to draw}) 42... Re8 43. h7 $4 {who would only say, advancing the passed pawn loses the game?} (43. Kf2 Ke5 44. Kf3 Kf5 45. Rg4 $11 {followed by Rg7 leads to a draw}) 43... Rh8 44. Kf2 Ke6 45. Ke3 Kf6 46. Kd3 Kg6 47. Rg4+ Kxh7 48. Ra4 Rd8+ 49. Kc4 Rd7 50. Kb5 Kg6 51. Ra1 Kf7 52. Kc6 Re7 53. Ra2 Ke8 54. Rd2 Re5 55. Ra2 Re6+ 56. Kb7 Re7+ 57. Ka6 Kd8 $4 {surprisingly misses the win!} (57... Re5 58. Ra1 Kd7 59. Ra2 Kc6 60. Ra3 Rc5 61. Ra1 Rc3 62. Ra2 b5 $19) 58. Rh2 Rc7 59. Rd2+ $2 (59. Rh6 Kd7 60. Rg6 $11 {would be the easy way to draw}) 59... Ke7 {now position is again lost for White.} 60. Rd3 Ke6 61. Rd2 Rd7 62. Rh2 Kd6 63. Rg2 Kc5 64. Rc2+ Kb4 65. Rb2+ Kc3 66. Rb1 (66. Rb5 {would be the best defense} Re7 $1 {a waiting move} 67. Rg5 (67. Rb1 Re5 $19) 67... Kd4 $19 {preparing ...-Re5}) 66... Rd5 $1 67. Ra1 (67. Kxa7 b5 $19) 67... Kb3 68. Rb1+ Ka2 69. Rc1 Ra5+ 70. Kb7 b5 71. Rc5 a6 72. Kb6 Ra4 0-1

