{[%evp 0,54,19,31,14,-14,-11,4,33,-27,0,1,0,0,-5,13,-9,3,28,25,76,40,33,5,11,0,-23,-26,-19,-31,-31,-39,-16,-16,-16,-5,9,12,17,17,-73,-8,0,0,0,0,0,0,-21,-21,-13,-5,-26,-26,-26,-26,-26]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 {The Nimzo-Indian. It was one of the most expected options from my opponent.} O-O $5 {Novelty in my games.} ({In all earlier games I'd go for} 4... d5 {trying to avoid the lines with 5.e4, however, for this game I made special prep.}) 5. e4 d5 6. e5 Ne4 7. Bd3 c5 8. a3 {A rare continuation. Can't say it didn't surprise me, as in the previous game Kirill had played 5.e4, he went for the main 8.Nf3 and that's what I was expecting the most from him, but surely I had something prepared also in here!} (8. Nf3 cxd4 9. Nxd4 Nd7 10. Bf4 {is what happened in Kirill's previous game} Ndc5 ({But I had} 10... Qh4 {in mind and after} 11. g3 {both} Qh3 ({and} 11... Qh5 {are the theoretical continuations.})) 11. O-O Bxc3 12. bxc3 Nxd3 13. Qxd3 Nc5 14. Qg3 Ne4 15. Qe3 $14 {1/2-1/2 (59) Shevchenko,K (2541)-Krishna Teja,N (2364) Barcelona 2019}) 8... Bxc3+ ({For the record, fun lines might happen in case of} 8... Ba5 9. Bxe4 dxe4 10. dxc5 Nc6 11. b4 Nxe5 {leads to weird positions} (11... Bc7 {is perhaps better}) 12. bxa5 Nd3+ 13. Kf1 f5 $44 {Black doesn't have even a pawn for the sacrificed piece, however, engine's evaluation is surprisingly low (for given material balance)}) 9. bxc3 Nc6 ({alternative is} 9... Qa5 10. Ne2 cxd4 11. cxd5 exd5 12. f3 Nxc3 (12... f5 $6 13. exf6 Nxf6 14. Nxd4 Re8+ 15. Kf2 $16 {leads to better position for White, thanks to the Bishop pair}) 13. Nxd4 Ne4+ ({the natural} 13... Nb5+ {doesn't fully equalize due to} 14. Bd2 Nxd4 15. Bxh7+ Kh8 16. Bxa5 Nxc2+ 17. Bxc2 Nc6 18. Bb4 $5 Nxb4 19. axb4 $14 {and now White's pieces are more active as well as the King is closer to the center.}) 14. Ke2 f5 $1 15. e6 {leads to well known draw} (15. exf6 $5 {is perhaps a better practical try for White} Nxf6 16. Be3 Nc6 17. Nxc6 bxc6 18. Qxc6 d4 19. Bxd4 Re8+ 20. Kf1 $36) 15... Nc6 16. Nxc6 bxc6 17. e7 (17. Qxc6 Rb8 18. e7 $4 Rb2+ $19) 17... Re8 18. Qxc6 Rxe7 19. Qxa8 Ng3+ 20. Kd1 Qc3 (20... Qa4+ 21. Kd2 Qa5+ 22. Kc2 Qa4+) 21. Bd2 Qxa1+ 22. Bc1 Qc3 23. Bd2 Qa1+ 24. Bc1 Qc3 25. Bd2 {1/2-1/2 (25) Wang,H (2737)-Wei,Y (2741) Danzhou 2019}) 10. Ne2 (10. f3 $2 {looks tempting but is just bad} Qh4+ 11. g3 Nxg3 12. Qf2 {looks like winning for White, but Black has important intermezzo} dxc4 $1 (12... Nf5 $4 {loses} 13. Bxf5 Qxf2+ 14. Kxf2 exf5 15. cxd5 $18) 13. Be4 (13. Bxc4 Nf5 $19) 13... Nf5 14. Qxh4 Nxh4 15. Bxc6 bxc6 16. dxc5 Ng6 17. f4 f6 18. exf6 Rxf6 19. Ne2 e5 20. fxe5 Nxe5 21. Be3 Rb8 22. Nd4 Nd3+ 23. Kd2 Rb2+ 24. Nc2 Rg6 25. Rhg1 Bf5 26. Rxg6 Bxg6 27. Kd1 Ne5 28. Rc1 Bh5+ {0-1 (28) Kahn,E (2358) -Brunello,S (2475) Budapest 2008}) (10. Nf3 $5 {is another try} cxd4 11. Bxe4 (11. cxd5 $6 exd5 12. cxd4 $2 Bg4 $17) 11... dxe4 12. Qxe4 dxc3 13. Ng5 {White tries to attack the "weakened" King as soon as possible, however, with the limited pieces it's hard to reach the rivalry King.} g6 14. Be3 {preventing ...-Qd4} (14. h4 Qd4 $15) (14. Qh4 h5 $17 {[%csl Re5]}) 14... Qa5 $5 15. Qh4 c2+ (15... h5 16. g4 c2+ 17. Ke2 {transposes to the main line}) 16. Ke2 h5 17. g4 {transposes to the main line} Qc3 $5 (17... Nxe5 18. gxh5 $13) 18. gxh5 Rd8 19. f3 Nd4+ 20. Bxd4 Rxd4 21. Ne4 Qxc4+ 22. Kf2 Qc5 23. Nxc5 Rxh4 24. hxg6 fxg6 $11 {with roughly equal position}) 10... cxd4 ({after} 10... f5 $2 11. cxd5 exd5 12. Qa2 $16 {things go horribly wrong for Black}) 11. cxd5 ({in case of} 11. cxd4 {Black plays} Qa5+ 12. Kf1 f6 $5 ({old main line is} 12... Nb4 13. axb4 Qxa1 {but normally in such positions, despite the material deficit, thanks to the activity of White pieces they get dynamic initiative.} 14. f3 f5 15. Qb1 $5 (15. Ke1 a5 16. cxd5 exd5 17. Qb3 Ng5 18. Qxd5+ Be6 19. Qxb7 axb4) 15... Qa4 16. Ke1 (16. Bc2 Qa6 $44) 16... a5 17. fxe4 fxe4 18. Bc2 Qxb4+ 19. Qxb4 axb4 20. cxd5 exd5 21. Bb3 Be6 22. Rf1 Rxf1+ 23. Kxf1 Ra1 24. Kf2 Kf7 25. h3 Rb1 26. Ba2 Ra1 27. Bb3 Rb1 28. Bc2 Ra1 29. Ke3 g5 30. Bb3 Rb1 31. Ba2 Ra1 32. Bb3 Rb1 33. Bc2 Ra1 34. Kd2 Bd7 35. Bb3 Ke6 36. Kc2 Bb5 37. Kb2 Ra3 38. Nc3 Rxb3+ 39. Kxb3 bxc3 40. Bxg5 Bf1 41. Kxc3 Bxg2 42. h4 Kd7 43. Kb4 Kc6 44. e6 Bh3 45. e7 Bd7 46. Ka5 b6+ 47. Ka6 b5 48. Ka5 Kd6 49. Kb4 Be8 50. Kc3 Ke6 51. Kb4 Kd6 52. Kc3 h6 53. Bxh6 Kxe7 54. Kb4 Kd6 55. Bc1 Kc6 56. Kc3 Bh5 57. Kb4 Be2 58. Be3 Kd6 59. h5 {1/2-1/2 (59) Ivanchuk,V (2716)-Navara,D (2602) Antalya 2004 CBM 101 [Golod,V]}) 13. Bxe4 dxe4 14. exf6 Rxf6 15. Be3 b5 $13) 11... exd5 12. cxd4 {transposes to the game} Bg4 $1 (12... Qa5+ {this time leads to slightly inferior endgame for Black} 13. Bd2 Qxd2+ 14. Qxd2 Nxd2 15. Kxd2 f6 16. exf6 (16. f4 Bg4 17. Ke3 g5 $1 18. g3 fxe5 19. dxe5 d4+ $1 20. Nxd4 Nxd4 21. Kxd4 gxf4) 16... Rxf6 17. f3 Bf5 18. Bxf5 Rxf5 19. Rab1 {objectively the endgame should be drawable, but still some technical difficulties are there.}) 13. f3 (13. O-O {leads to easy equality} Bxe2 14. Qxe2 Nxd4 15. Qe3 Ne6 16. Bxe4 dxe4 17. Qxe4 Qd4 18. Qxd4 Nxd4 $11) 13... Bxf3 $1 (13... Qh4+ {looks tempting but is just bad} 14. g3 Nxg3 {due to the intermezzo} 15. Bg5 $1 Qxg5 16. Bxh7+ Kh8 17. hxg3 Bh5 18. Bf5 $18) 14. gxf3 Qh4+ 15. Ng3 Nxd4 (15... Nxg3 {was also possible} 16. Qf2 Ne7 17. Rb1 Ngf5 18. Qxh4 Nxh4 19. Rxb7 $132) 16. Qg2 $8 Nc5 $8 17. Bb1 Ncb3 ({it was important not to mix up with which Knight to attack the a1-Rook! Black simply loses after} 17... Ndb3 $4 18. O-O $1 Qd4+ (18... Nxa1 19. Nf5 $18) 19. Kh1 Nxa1 20. Nf5 Qxe5 21. Bb2 $18 {and Black loses either the Queen or get checkmated}) {Now White hasn't got anything better than} 18. Bb2 Nxa1 19. Bxa1 f6 $1 {the last important move. After this I neutralize any kind initiative of White} (19... Rac8 $2 20. O-O $18 {followed by f4 is devastating}) 20. Ba2 (20. exf6 {possible but looking extremely risky} Rae8+ 21. Kd1 Rxf6 22. Qd2 Rb6 23. Ba2 Ne6 24. Re1 Qa4+ 25. Ke2 Nf4+ 26. Kf2 Rxe1 27. Kxe1 Re6+ $44) 20... Rac8 21. O-O Rc2 22. Bxd5+ Kh8 {leads to what we had in the game} 23. Rf2 Rc1+ 24. Rf1 Rc2 25. Rf2 Rc1+ 26. Rf1 Rc2 27. Rf2 Rc1+ {It doesn't happen often that you don't make even a move of yours but follow the home prep.} 1/2-1/2

