{[%evp 0,53,20,16,39,51,51,45,51,51,46,46,29,5,-2,6,6,7,8,18,15,7,46,42,38,7,0,0,0,4,21,26,26,-5,-5,-17,2,2,2,5,2,2,18,30,20,0,65,59,59,66,65,65,60,60,60,60]} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 {The Four Knights variation of the Sicilian Defense. It's become extremely popular lately and I myself have had already quite some games in this variation. İn general, it might be a risky choice to play in KO tournament, however, I was confident in the line...} 6. Nxc6 {entering the critical variations} bxc6 7. e5 Nd5 8. Ne4 Qc7 9. f4 Qb6 10. c4 Bb4+ 11. Ke2 f5 ({the old main line} 11... Ba6 $2 {leads to difficult positions for Black after} 12. Kf3 $16) 12. Nf2 {has become more popular lately} ({Undoubtedly, the main move is} 12. exf6) 12... Ba6 13. Kf3 Ne7 14. Be3 Bc5 15. Bxc5 Qxc5 16. Qa4 $5 {This came as a surprise for me. I had never seen this earlier, and immediately I figured out that my opponent has outprepared me.} (16. Qd6 {was the only move I had analyzed, after what both} Qb6 ({and} 16... Qa5 {are quite playable})) 16... Bb7 {Played after 6 minutes of thinking. Other moves didn't make much sense to me} ({on} 16... Qb6 {I disliked} 17. c5 Bb5 {is a move I did not focus on too much} (17... Qxb2 $2 18. Bxa6 $18 {and now a1-Rook is defended}) 18. Qxb5 Qxb5 19. Bxb5 cxb5 20. h4 (20. Rhd1 Rc8 ({also} 20... g5 $132 {looks OK for Black.}) 21. b4 Nd5 22. Rxd5 {gives nothing to White} exd5 23. a4 bxa4 24. Rxa4 $44) 20... a5 {according to engine White's slightly better, on the other hand, Black's quite solid.}) 17. h4 $1 $146 {Official novelty. Otherwise ... -g5 was coming} Qb6 (17... O-O-O 18. Rd1 $16) (17... d5 18. exd6 Qxd6 19. Rd1 Qc7 20. c5 $16 {[%csl Ye5][%cal Gf1c4,Yf2d3,Yd3e5] looked promising for White}) (17... a5 {trying to prevent b4 looked reasonable, but I couldn't see the follow up after} 18. Rd1 $36) ({I considered} 17... O-O 18. Rd1 Rfd8 {and if} 19. b4 $2 ({but wasn't sure how to play in case of} 19. Be2 Ng6 20. Nd3 Qd4 21. Kg3 (21. h5 Qe4+ 22. Kg3 Qxe2 23. hxg6 c5 $19) 21... d6 22. exd6 Rxd6 (22... e5) 23. Bf3 (23. h5 e5 $1 {[%cal Gd6g6]}) 23... h5) {I have a nice reaction} 19... Qb5 $1 {and Black is even better} (19... Qb6 {leads to much better position for White} 20. c5 Qc7 {due to} 21. Qa5 $1 Qxa5 22. bxa5 Rab8 23. Bc4 Kf7 (23... Bc8 24. Bxe6+) (23... Ba8) 24. Rb1 {and after a6 Black will be totally paralyzed with the Bishop on -a8})) 18. Qa3 {Played instantly again, defending on b2 and perhaps preparing c5. Of course, it puts extra pressure on you, when you know that you're following your opponent's prep.} c5+ {played after 7 minutes of thinking.} 19. Kg3 O-O $2 {Played nearly instantly. This natural move costed me the match and the elimination from the World Cup.} (19... O-O-O $2 {is bad due to} 20. Nd3 $16 {and I cannot defend the c5-pawn}) (19... d6 {looks natural but weakens -e5 square} 20. exd6 Qxd6 21. Nd3 $16 {and again White's dominating}) (19... h5 {trying to bring the Rook to -g6 doesn't help Black either} 20. Rd1 Rh6 21. Rh3 Rg6+ 22. Kh2 Qc6 23. Rg3 $18 {and Black has ocean of weaknesses}) (19... a5 {with the idea of playing ...-Qb4 also crossed my mind} 20. Rd1 Qb4 21. Qd3 (21. Nd3 $5 Qxa3 22. bxa3 Rc8 $132) (21. Qe3 $5 Qxb2 22. Be2 $44) 21... Bc6 22. Rd2 {[%cal Ga5a3,Gb2b3]} O-O 23. Be2 {but I wasn't sure about this position}) ({Perhaps the best reaction was} 19... h6 $5 {preparing ...-Rg8 and ...-g5 playing on White King's situation! I didn't even think about this possibillity. ..} 20. Nd3 {attacks the c5-pawn} (20. Rd1 O-O-O $5 21. Nd3 (21. Rd6 $6 {is just bad due to} Qb4 $1 22. Qxb4 (22. Qxa7 Nc6 23. Qa3 Qxa3+ 24. bxa3 g5 $17 {and Black is taking the initiative!}) 22... cxb4 23. Nd3 g5 24. hxg5 $6 hxg5 25. Rxh8 Rxh8 26. fxg5 f4+ $1 27. Kg4 (27. Nxf4 Nf5+ 28. Kf2 Nxd6 29. exd6 Rf8 $19) 27... Ng6 {threatening ...-Rh4 checkmate} 28. g3 f3 $17 {leads to difficult position for White.}) 21... g5 $1 22. hxg5 (22. Nxc5 gxf4+ 23. Kh2) 22... hxg5 23. Rxh8 gxf4+ 24. Kxf4 Ng6+ 25. Kg5 Rxh8 26. Qxc5+ (26. Kxg6 Qd8 $18) 26... Qxc5 27. Nxc5 Nxe5 $132) 20... Rc8 {I have to defend it.} 21. Be2 Rg8 $5 {preparing ...-g5} 22. Rad1 Be4 $132) 20. Rd1 $16 {now White has total control over the situation as Black fails to create any kind counterplay.} Rfd8 21. Be2 (21. Rd6 Bc6) 21... Rab8 {my plan was to wait until Andrey plays Rd6 and meet it with ...-Bc6. The idea is, in this scenario White cannot play Nd3 due to ... -Nc8 that traps the d6-Rook.} (21... Nc6 22. Rd6 $18) (21... Nc8 22. Rd3 d6 23. Rhd1 $16) (21... d6 {leads to strategically losing position} 22. exd6 Rxd6 23. Rxd6 Qxd6 24. Rd1 Qb6 (24... Qc7 25. Qe3 $18) 25. Nd3 Rc8 26. h5 $18) 22. Rd2 $1 {the most precise} (22. Rd6 Bc6 23. b3 (23. Nd3 $2 Nc8 $17) 23... a5 {[%cal Ga5a4]} 24. Rhd1 (24. Nd3 $2 Nc8) 24... a4 25. bxa4 Qa5 $132) 22... Nc6 (22... d6 {perhaps would be a better try} 23. exd6 Rxd6 24. Rxd6 Qxd6 25. Rd1 Qb6 26. h5 {covering g6-square} e5 $1 (26... Nc6 $2 27. Rd6 $18) 27. fxe5 Re8 $5 (27... Qc7 28. Nd3 f4+ 29. Kh2 Nf5 30. Qxc5) (27... f4+ 28. Kh2 Nf5) 28. Nd3 f4+ 29. Nxf4 (29. Kh2 $2 f3 $1 $132) 29... Nf5+ 30. Kh2 Rxe5 31. Qd3 $8 Nd4 32. Bg4 $16 {surely, at the end of the variation White's better, but it was far from easy!}) 23. Rd6 $1 {Here I hated my position so much that I simply stopped fighting. After the next played move my position become hopeless} Qb4 $2 (23... Ba8 24. Nd3 $18) (23... Nd4 24. Bd1 Qc7 (24... Bc6 25. Nd3 $18) 25. Nd3 Rbc8 26. Qxc5 $18) ({Meanwhile after the engine's top choice} 23... Qa5 $1 {things are not that elementary!} 24. Qxa5 Nxa5 {[%cal Gb7c6,Ga5b7] followed by ...-Bc6 and ...-Nb7} 25. Rhd1 Bc6 26. b3 Nb7 27. R6d2 a5 28. Bf3 {The light-squared Bishop of Black is key piece in this position as it defends the d7-pawn} Bxf3 29. Kxf3 a4 {leads to an endgame where White has extra pawn but Black has some practical chances for a draw.}) 24. Qxb4 $1 {now the game is technically won for White.} (24. Nd3 $2 {would of course be a blunder due to} Qd2 25. Bf3 Nd4 {and White is the side that has to be careful here}) 24... Nxb4 (24... cxb4 25. Rhd1 $18 {and I cannot defend the d7-pawn}) 25. a3 $1 Nc2 26. Rc1 $1 Nd4 27. Bd1 $1 Be4 28. b4 Kf8 29. Rc3 $1 {with the precise moves played back to back, the game is over now.} ({It's never late to go wrong as had White played} 29. bxc5 $2 Nb3 $36 {suddenly Black is the side to have the initiative!}) 29... Ke7 (29... cxb4 30. axb4 Nc6 31. b5 Ne7 32. Ra3 Rbc8 33. Rd4 {[AbasovN]} (33. Be2)) 30. bxc5 Nc6 31. Nxe4 fxe4 32. Bc2 e3 33. Rxe3 Na5 34. Ra6 Rb2 35. Bxh7 Nxc4 36. Rc3 Nd2 37. Bc2 {Smooth victory by Andrey.} 1-0

