{[%evp 0,64,22,29,20,5,-6,-18,-20,-20,-6,-3,8,22,13,2,-15,-29,-23,-34,59,55,45,-7,-15,-11,6,8,0,-30,-5,-7,18,36,33,65,51,49,63,18,6,37,8,40,51,33,14,24,26,39,18,8,23,19,12,6,0,6,21,13,11,20,4,41,10,10,21]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. g3 d5 4. Bg2 Be7 5. Nf3 O-O 6. O-O dxc4 7. Qc2 a6 8. Rd1 $5 {Came as a surprise for me. We can call it Dubov's variation as he put enormous effort on making this line great again.} b5 9. Ne5 c6 (9... Nd5 {was the alternative} 10. b3 Bb7 (10... cxb3 11. axb3 Bb7 12. Ba3 Bxa3 13. Nxa3 Nd7 14. Nc6 Bxc6 15. Qxc6 N7b6 16. e4 Nb4 17. Qc3 Qe7 18. h4 $44 {1-0 (27) Dubov,D (2699)-Vidit,S (2726) Chess.com INT 2020}) 11. bxc4 Nb4 12. Qc3 Bxg2 13. Kxg2 c5 14. Bb2 Bf6 15. Qf3 Nd7 16. Nxd7 Qxd7 17. Nc3 cxd4 18. Ne4 Be5 19. e3 Nc6 $132 {1/2-1/2 (30) Belous,V (2587)-Brown,M (2489) Chicago 2017 CBM 179 [Belous,V]}) 10. b3 {Typical move for such kind positions} (10. Nxc6 $2 Nxc6 11. Bxc6 Ra7 12. a4 b4 $1 $146 13. Bf4 (13. Qxc4 $4 Rc7 $19 {and White cannot unpin}) 13... Nd5 $17 {looks great for Black}) 10... cxb3 11. axb3 Bb7 12. Nc3 ({in case of} 12. Ba3 {like once Dubov played I was going to play} Bxa3 13. Rxa3 (13. Nxa3 {Knight looks a bit misplaced on a3}) 13... a5 $132) 12... Nbd7 ({on} 12... Qc8 {with the idea of playing ...-c5, White has strong} 13. Ne4 $1 Nxe4 14. Bxe4 h6 15. Bf4 $14) 13. Bg5 {In this position I had extra half an hour and was rather happy about the opening outcome. Complex position, yes, less space yet an extra pawn} (13. Bxc6 Qc8 $36) (13. Ne4 Nxe4 14. Bxe4 Nxe5 15. dxe5 Qc7 16. Bxh7+ Kh8 17. Be4 c5 $132) 13... Rc8 14. Nd3 Nd5 $6 {positional blunder} (14... Qb6 15. b4 h6 16. Bxf6 Nxf6 17. e3 $36) ({Perhaps I should have challenged the g5-Bishop} 14... h6 15. Bxf6 Nxf6 16. Nc5 Qc7 17. b4 Nd5 $44) 15. Bxe7 Qxe7 16. Ne4 Nb4 17. Nxb4 Qxb4 18. Qd2 $1 {While playing 14...-Nd5 I overlooked this move} (18. Nc5 Nxc5 19. dxc5 Rfd8 20. Rxd8+ Rxd8 21. Rd1 Rxd1+ 22. Qxd1 Qxc5 23. Qd7 Ba8 24. Qc8+ Qf8 25. Qxa6 $11 {is what I calculated}) 18... Qe7 ({problem with} 18... Qxd2 19. Rxd2 {is that the d6-square is too weak passive} Rc7 (19... c5 $4 20. Nd6 $18) 20. Nd6 Nb8 21. e4 Rd8 22. e5 Rxd6 23. exd6 Rd7 {could have maybe be played?!}) 19. b4 {here I had hard times finding reasonable continuation, and my time advantage slowly started to melt away.} (19. Qf4 $5 {[%cal Ge4d6,Yf4d6]} e5 20. dxe5 Nxe5 21. Nd6 Rc7 22. Nxb7 Rxb7 23. Rxa6 $14) 19... Rfd8 (19... f5 20. Nc5 Nxc5 21. dxc5 Rfd8 22. Qf4 $16 {looked very ugly}) 20. Qf4 Nb6 21. Nc5 Nc4 {yes, a bit astray, but around here I started to play a bit faster, trying to get out of position thanks to my opponent's time trouble} 22. h4 {AlphaZero pawn in the action!} Ra8 {sort of preparing ...-a5. But do I?!} 23. Qg5 $5 ({on} 23. h5 {surely I wouldn't let White pawn reach h6 and myself would play} h6) 23... f6 24. Qg4 $6 {perhaps this was an inaccuracy} (24. Qc1 $5 Rd6 25. e3 {followed by Bf1 looked stronger}) 24... Rd6 {defending e6-pawn and now Queen has not much to do on g4} 25. h5 Rad8 (25... h6 {was also an option, however, I didn't want to weaken complex light-squares around my King.}) 26. h6 g6 (26... Bc8 $5 {looked reasonable}) 27. e3 e5 {Using the fact that my opponent gets less and less time, I tried to keep position as dynamic as possible.} 28. Qf3 Bc8 29. Ne4 (29. Nxa6 f5 30. Nc5 e4 31. Qe2 g5 {looked totally unclear!} 32. Qh5 Rg6 $13) 29... Re6 30. Nc5 ({a try to play for something more than a draw by White} 30. dxe5 {doesn't lead to much} Nxe5 31. Qe2 Rxd1+ 32. Rxd1 Nf7 33. Nc5 Rd6 34. Rxd6 Qxd6 {and the final position looks ok for Black}) 30... Red6 31. Ne4 Re6 32. Nc5 Red6 {And the game ended in a draw with three-fold repetition. After the game I had a bitter aftertaste in a view of not really having played a good game, but, I was happy about managing to keep the position dynamic enough while lacking space and survive!} 1/2-1/2

