1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 d5 3. c4 e6 4. g3 dxc4 5. Bg2 c5 {honestly speaking, I wasn't expecting this line of Catalan from my opponent} 6. O-O Nc6 {after some while of thinking I decided to enter the easiest in practice variation} 7. dxc5 (7. Ne5 Bd7 8. Na3 {was the alternative I was thinking about} cxd4 9. Naxc4 {and here Black has three reasonable continuations (9...-Bc5; 9...-Be7 and 9... -Rc8). Since I wasn't remembering the precise reactions in all cases, my decision of not entering the most principled lines make sense.}) 7... Qxd1 8. Rxd1 Bxc5 9. Nbd2 c3 10. bxc3 O-O 11. Nb3 (11. Ne1 {is another interesting try by White where I already had sorrowful experience} Rd8 12. Nd3 Be7 13. Nb3 Bd7 (13... Nd5 $5 14. Bd2 Nb6 $132) 14. Nbc5 Na5 15. Nxb7 Nxb7 16. Bxb7 Rab8 17. Bf3 Ba4 18. Re1 Rbc8 19. Be3 Rxc3 20. Bxa7 Ba3 21. Bb6 {and eventually my opponent managed to convert the material advantage 1-0 (40) Kuzubov,Y (2667) -Abasov,N (2527) Abu Dhabi 2014}) 11... Be7 12. c4 Bd7 13. Bb2 Rfd8 14. Nfd4 Rac8 15. Nb5 Be8 $2 {huge positional mistake} (15... b6 {is important move here} 16. Nd6 ({in case of} 16. Rac1 {my compatrion Mamedyarov illustrated correct reaction} a5 $1 17. a4 Be8 18. Rxd8 Rxd8 19. h3 Nb4 20. c5 bxc5 21. Nxa5 Nfd5 22. Nb7 Rb8 23. Nxc5 Bxb5 24. axb5 Rxb5 $11 {and the game soon ended in a draw ½-½ (46) Andreikin,D (2709)-Mamedyarov,S (2757) Khanty-Mansiysk 2014 CBM 160 [Marin,M]}) 16... Bxd6 (16... Rc7 17. Ba3 {would be my try} (17. Nb5 Rcc8 {leads to repetition}) 17... Ne5 (17... Kf8 18. c5 Nb8 $4 19. Nb5 $18 {and Black's position is hopeless: 1-0 (37) Cheparinov,I (2681)-Ponomariov,R (2717) Tromsoe 2014 CBM 162 [Marin,M]}) 18. Nb5 Bxb5 19. Rxd8+ Bxd8 20. cxb5 Be7 21. Bxe7 Rxe7 22. Rc1 g5) 17. Rxd6 Be8 {I assume this is what my opponent mixed up with}) 16. c5 $1 {It's surprising how just one inaccurate move costs so much trouble! Now Black is having tough time due to d6-square.} Nd5 17. Nd6 $1 Bxd6 18. cxd6 Nb6 (18... Rxd6 $4 19. e4 $18) 19. Rac1 {preventing ...-Nc4} Na4 20. Ba1 (20. Ba3 {was also an option}) 20... f6 21. f4 $1 {Typical move, grabbing more space and bringing King closer towards the center} Bd7 22. Kf2 Kf7 23. Bf3 b6 24. Rd2 $6 {perhaps this was a bit slow} ({The advantage of Bishops over Knights is - even from the edge of the board - the a1-square Bishop can be almighty in the attack!} 24. g4 $1 {followed by h4-g5. Black's position falls appart!}) 24... Ke8 25. g4 (25. Rdc2 Nb4 26. Rc7 Nxa2 27. R1c2 Nb4 28. R2c4 {seems to be strong for White} Nd5 29. f5 $1 $18) 25... Nb4 $1 26. Rg1 $2 {I totally underestimated Black's next move.} (26. Rc7 $5 Rxc7 27. dxc7 Rc8 28. g5 $18) ({or} 26. g5 Rxc1 27. Nxc1 Rc8 28. Nd3 $18) 26... Nc2 $1 {And now it turns out that my 'almighty' Bishop on -a1 is being traded off. Also, with the last couple moves Black opened the c-file for the Rook. Here I got exteremly nervous as I was realizing I totally spoiled my wonderful position. Nevertheless, I still was keen to push as much as I could} 27. g5 $5 (27. Rxc2 {is the suggestion of the engine} Rxc2 28. g5 fxg5 29. Bxg7 Bc6 30. Nd4 Bxf3 31. Nxc2 Bd5 32. Be5 Nc5 {and still it looks like Black is about to stabilyze!}) 27... Nxa1 28. gxf6 Nxb3 29. axb3 Kf7 $2 ({I really am not sure how Igor didn't go for simple} 29... gxf6 30. bxa4 Kf8 {and Black is totally safe!} 31. Rg3 {followed by Rd1-g1, can be met by} Rc1 $132) ({Meanwhile, Black couldn't save the Knight with} 29... Nc5 $4 {in a view of} 30. Rxg7 {and Black's King is totally exposed.} Nxb3 31. Rd1 Rc1 (31... a5 32. Bh5+ Kf8 33. Rdg1 $18) 32. Rxc1 Nxc1 33. Rg8+ $18) 30. bxa4 Kxf6 31. e4 $1 {and Black cannot stop the e5 move, after what White's central pawns will be too strong} g6 (31... e5 32. fxe5+ Kxe5 33. Rxg7 $18) (31... Bxa4 32. e5+ Kf7 33. f5) 32. e5+ Kg7 33. Ke3 Bxa4 34. h4 {Not sure if it was necessary, but I wanted to keep attacking the Black King!} Rc3+ 35. Rd3 Rc5 $2 {loses immediately} (35... Rxd3+ 36. Kxd3 Bb5+ 37. Ke3 Rc8 {could have been a better practical try, even though position still should be objectively losing after} 38. Be4 $18) 36. Ra1 b5 37. Bd1 $1 {was overlooked by Kovalenko} Kf7 (37... Bxd1 38. Rxa7+ $18) (37... Rc4 38. Bxa4 bxa4 39. Rd4 Rc3+ 40. Ke4 a3 41. Ra4 {and White wins}) 38. Bxa4 bxa4 39. Rxa4 {material balance was finally restored, but my pawns are much more advanced and powerful} a5 40. Rb3 {taking open file} Rd7 41. Kd4 Rd5+ 42. Kc4 {covering b5-square} h6 (42... Rd8 43. Rb7+ Kg8 44. Kc3) 43. Rd3 {forced Rook trades. The rest is easy} ({or} 43. Rb5 $18) 43... Rxd3 44. Kxd3 Ra7 45. Kc4 Ke8 46. Kb5 Rb7+ 47. Kc6 Ra7 48. Kb6 {I was really happy to finish the tournament with a win against the top seed of the event and a very strong GM!} 1-0

