1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Qb6 {Grivas' variation. I was actually happy to see this happening on board, cause, before the game instead of resting, I spent most of my time to prepare against it:)} 5. Nb3 Nf6 6. Nc3 e6 7. Qe2 $5 {I believe, 7.Qe2 is the most principle line in Grivas' variation} ({However, White has numerous reasonable alternatives such as} 7. a3 $5 {which is also worth to try}) 7... Bb4 8. Bd2 O-O 9. a3 Be7 ({in our days} 9... Bxc3 10. Bxc3 e5 {is becoming popular}) 10. O-O-O d5 ({The first game I met the idea of 7.Qe2 was between Anand and Kramnik. This game inspired me to learn this variation!} 10... d6 11. g4 a6 12. g5 Nd7 13. h4 Qc7 14. f4 b5 15. Kb1 b4 16. axb4 Nxb4 17. f5 Rb8 18. Bf4 exf5 19. exf5 Ne5 20. Bh3 Re8 21. g6 hxg6 22. Bxe5 dxe5 23. fxg6 fxg6 24. h5 Bf5 25. hxg6 Bxh3 26. Rxh3 Bg5 27. Ne4 Bf4 28. Nbc5 {1-0 (28) Anand,V (2790)-Kramnik,V (2795) Mainz 2001}) 11. e5 Ne8 ({I was more familiar with the move} 11... Nd7 {as I played myself this line a year earlier} 12. f4 a6 13. Qh5 Qc7 14. Bd3 {and I failed to succeed} g6 15. Qh6 Re8 16. Ne2 b5 17. Kb1 Rb8 18. h4 Qd8 19. Ned4 {...1-0, Korneev,O - Abasov, N (Baku, 2013)}) 12. f4 f6 13. exf6 ({relatively the main line is} 13. Be3 Qc7 14. exf6 Bxf6 15. Nb5 Qb8 16. g3 $36 {and it looks better for White}) 13... Bxf6 14. g3 {[%csl Re6][%cal Gf1h3,Gh3e6] now we have reached more or less French type structure, Which certainly should be in favor of White, thanks to the pawn structure and light squared Bishop} Nd6 15. Kb1 $5 $146 {quite an interesting novelty over board, the idea of this move is well understandable in the game text} ({earlier} 15. Bh3 {was tried mostly} Re8 16. Kb1 Nd4 $13 {but occured position doesn't look much promising for White}) (15. Be3 {happened in a quite dramatic thriller game between Hamdouchi,H - Korneev,O which ended with surprising Black's victory in an endgame being Queen down!} Qc7 16. Qe1 Kh8 17. Kb1 a6 18. Bc5 Bd7 19. Bxd6 Qxd6 20. Ne4 Qe7 21. Nxf6 Qxf6 22. Nc5 Rad8 23. Bh3 Rfe8 24. Rf1 Bc8 25. Nd3 Qh6 26. Bg4 e5 27. Bxc8 e4 28. Bxb7 exd3 29. Qc3 dxc2+ 30. Qxc2 Ne7 31. Rfe1 Qd6 32. Qa4 d4 33. Bxa6 Rf8 34. Qb4 Qxa6 35. Rxe7 Qf6 36. Ka1 Rb8 37. Rb7 Rxb7 38. Qxb7 Rd8 39. Qe4 h6 40. Qd3 Qb6 41. Qc4 Qd6 42. Rd3 Kh7 43. f5 Kh8 44. Qe6 Qf8 45. f6 gxf6 46. Qb6 Rd6 47. Qa7 Qe8 48. Qa5 Qe5 49. Qa8+ Kg7 50. Qb7+ Kg6 51. Qf3 h5 52. h4 Qe1+ 53. Rd1 Qe5 54. g4 hxg4 55. Qxg4+ Kf7 56. Rg1 Rd8 57. Qg6+ Ke6 58. Qb1 d3 59. Re1 d2 60. Rxe5+ fxe5 61. Qd1 e4 62. Kb1 e3 63. Kc2 Rc8+ 64. Kd3 Rc1 65. Ke2 Kf5 66. h5 Kg5 67. a4 Kf4 68. h6 Ke4 {0-1 (68) Hamdouchi,H (2576)-Korneev,O (2565) Sort 2007}) 15... Nc4 16. Qe1 $1 {now since I have my Bishop yet undeveloped, I'm intending to take on c4 and play Be3 followed by Nd4} Nxd2+ ({probably the strongest was} 16... Rd8 $5 17. Bxc4 ({doesn't look much convincing} 17. Bc1 {due to} N6a5) 17... dxc4 18. Be3 Rxd1+ 19. Nxd1 Qd8 $1 (19... Qb5 20. Nc5 $14) 20. Nc5 Qd5 $1 {[%csl Rh1]} 21. Bf2 Nd4 22. Ne4 Nf3 23. Ndc3 Nxe1 24. Nxd5 exd5 25. Nxf6+ gxf6 26. Rxe1 Kf7 $11 {should be equal}) ({the idea of 15.Kb1 is that on} 16... N6a5 {I had prepared strong nuance} 17. Nxd5 $1 {and there are no checks from b3 or b2} exd5 18. Bxa5 $16) 17. Qxd2 {now position more or less has clarified and White's initiative is clear} Ne7 $6 {Black went too passive here} ({He couldn't play} 17... Na5 $2 {rightaway, due to the same tactical nuance} 18. Nxd5 $1 Nxb3 19. Nxf6+ Rxf6 20. cxb3 Qxb3 21. Bg2 $16 {and Black's position simply collapsed}) ({Black should have prepared Na5 with} 17... Kh8 $5 {to avoid checks from f6} 18. Bh3 (18. Na4 $5 Qc7 19. Bh3 e5 20. Bxc8 Raxc8 21. Nc3 exf4 22. Nxd5 Qe5 23. Nxf6 Qxf6 24. gxf4 Qxf4 $11) 18... Na5 19. Nxa5 Qxa5 20. Ne4 Qxd2 21. Nxd2 a5 22. Rhe1 Ra6 23. Nf3 Rb6 24. Ne5 {White surely has some edge} g5 {But Black has their own counter-play}) 18. Bh3 Bd7 $6 ({Black should have start attacking quickly, otherwise their position strategically is seriously worse} 18... a5 $5 19. a4 $5 {is reasonable answer but now} Bd7 {wins on power, as at least it targets in some lines the 'a4' pawn} 20. Rhe1 Rac8 21. Ne4 Bxa4 $5 {and even here White has the initiative} 22. Qf2 Rc6 23. Qxb6 Rxb6 24. Nec5 Bxb3 25. cxb3 $36) 19. Rhe1 {so far I'm just improving position of my pieces} Rac8 20. Ne4 $1 $16 {probably my opponent blundered this nuance playing ...-Bd7, or I don't know} dxe4 $2 $18 (20... Rc4 21. Nxf6+ Rxf6 22. Nd4 $16) 21. Qxd7 Nf5 22. Bxf5 exf5 23. Qxf5 {now White's position is completely winning} e3 ({no discover attacks are possible} 23... Bxb2 {due to an intermediate check} 24. Qd5+ $18) 24. Qa5 ({stronger was} 24. Rd7 $18 {[%cal Gf5d3]}) 24... Qe6 (24... Qxa5 25. Nxa5 b6 26. Nb3 Rce8 {[%cal Gg7g5]} 27. Rd3 g5 28. Rdxe3 Rxe3 29. Rxe3 gxf4 30. gxf4 Kf7 {[%cal Gf7g6,Gg6f5] probably should be still winning, but Black is still in the game here}) 25. Qd5 $18 {now the game is over} ({innaccurate} 25. Qxa7 $2 {is met by} Rxc2) 25... Rce8 26. c3 h5 27. Nc5 Qxd5 28. Rxd5 Rf7 (28... Re7 29. Rd7) 29. Kc2 (29. Rxh5 {was also possible}) 29... g5 30. fxg5 Bg7 31. Nd3 Re4 32. Nf4 {generally speaking, my opponent didn't make a super blunder, however, structurally his position was getting worse and worse after each move. He should have looked for counterplay on the Queenside what he didn't do, and blundered Ne4 motiff after what Black's position got even worse, and ever since then my play was much easier} 1-0

