1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 {It was the first surprise for my opponent, he mostly was expecting from me Berlin what I tried couple of times earlier in this tournament} 4. Ba4 Nge7 $5 {Deffered Cozio. I have never played this opening but I prepared it a year ago} 5. c3 g6 6. d4 exd4 7. cxd4 b5 8. Bc2 d5 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. O-O Be6 {I think we can call this move the Azerbaijani line:) 3 Azeri GMs (!) has developed the theory in this line. Besides me regularly Mamedyarov and Durarbeyli used this variation in their arsenal} 11. Bg5 {by playing} Be7 {I wanted to understand my opponent's mood, whether he is ready for a fight or not. Also it was a novelty in classical games, although only once Durarbayli played this move in Blitz World Championship in 2014 against Najer} 12. Bh6 (12. Bxe7 $5 $146 {Looks very interesting} Ndxe7 (12... Ncxe7 13. Re1 O-O 14. Nbd2 $36 {[%cal Gd2e4]}) 13. Nc3 O-O 14. Re1 Bg4 15. d5 Bxf3 16. gxf3 $132) 12... Bf8 13. Qd2 {he refuses the repitition} ({In case he'd play} 13. Bg5 {I'd follow Leko-Mamedyarov game with} Qd7 $5 14. a4 Rb8 15. Nbd2 Bg7 16. Ne4 O-O 17. h3 Ndb4 18. Nc5 Qd6 19. Nxe6 fxe6 20. axb5 Nxd4 21. Nxd4 Bxd4 22. Bb3 Rxf2 $132 {1/2 (42) Leko,P (2744)-Mamedyarov,S (2766) Zug 2013}) 13... Bxh6 14. Qxh6 Qd6 $146 {[%cal Gd6f4]} ({I remembered the ideas, putting pressure over d4 pawn and always keeping the idea of exchanging Queens with Qf4 but I simply forgot the accuracy} 14... Qf6 $1 {is much more precise}) 15. Nc3 (15. a4 $5 b4 16. Nbd2 O-O-O $132) 15... O-O-O 16. Be4 (16. Ne4 $6 Qf4 $15) 16... f5 $1 17. Bxd5 Bxd5 18. Nxd5 Qxd5 19. Rfd1 Rd7 20. a4 b4 {I was sort of happy with my position, despite not much pieces left on board, the position seemed for me to be quite sharp and it requires accurate play from both players. That was the main idea why I chose Deffered Cozio} 21. Rac1 Re8 22. Qd2 {By now, I was playing quick and confidently. Here I felt some danger and spent quite a long time on finding the correct path to keep the balance in position} Re4 ({another reasonable option was} 22... Re6 23. Qd3 Kb7 24. Rc4 {but I didn't see in my calculations} Na5 $1 25. Rxb4+ ({now White would have to admit wrongness of their last move and go back} 25. Rcc1 Rc6 $5 $36) 25... Ka7 {[%cal Gc7c5] followed by c5} 26. Qc3 $5 c5 27. Rb5 axb5 28. Qxa5+ Ra6 29. Qxb5 Rb7 $15 {with promising position for Black} (29... cxd4 $6 30. Nxd4 $11)) ({on} 22... Kb7 {follows strong} 23. b3 $1 {and now it's not clear what to do} Ka7 (23... Qxb3 {is worse due to} 24. d5 $16 {[%cal Gc1b1,Rb1b7] followed by Rb1}) 24. h3 $36 {and Black lost the harmony in the position}) 23. Qd3 Kb7 24. Rc4 g5 (24... Na5 $1 {Very strong accidental move. We both missed this brilliant idea. After 24... -Na5 Black takes the initiative! Now White has got 2 options, moving the Rook back, or pick the principle line and take on b4, both are good for Black} 25. Rxb4+ (25. Rcc1 c5 26. Rxc5 {otherwise c4 would follow} Qxc5 27. dxc5 Rxd3 28. Rxd3 Kc7 $1 $15 {[%csl Rc5][%cal Ga5b7,Gb7c5,Ge4e2]}) 25... Ka7 {[%csl Rb4] the rook is misplaced on b4} 26. Qd2 $1 (26. b3 c5 27. Rb6 Kxb6 28. dxc5+ Kc7 29. Qxd5 Rxd5 30. Rxd5 Nxb3 $17 {The endgame is in favor of Black due to the falling 'a' and 'c' pawns of White}) (26. Qc3 c5 27. Rb5 axb5 28. Qxa5+ Kb8 $36) 26... Nc6 27. Rc1 Nxb4 28. Qxb4 c5 29. Rxc5 Rb7 $36 {[%cal Gd5b3]}) 25. Rdc1 ({interesting was} 25. h3 $5 {now I would have to find} Na5 $1 {otherwise I'm worse} ({moves like} 25... h6 {lead to favorable Rook ending for White} 26. Rdc1 Nxd4 27. Rxb4+ Nb5 28. Qxd5+ Rxd5 29. Rxe4 fxe4 30. axb5 exf3 31. bxa6+ Kxa6 32. Rxc7 Rd1+ 33. Kh2 fxg2 34. Kxg2 Rd6 {Black can hold this or cannot. Position of White remains to be promising} 35. h4 $5) 26. Rxb4+ Ka7 27. Qc3 c5 28. Rb5 Nb7 $5 (28... axb5 29. Qxa5+ Kb8 30. Qxb5+ Rb7 31. Qxc5 Qxc5 32. dxc5 g4 33. Ng5 $11 {with drawish consequences}) 29. Rb3 g4 30. Ne1 Rxd4 31. Rxd4 Qxd4 32. hxg4 fxg4 $132) 25... Nxd4 $8 26. Rxb4+ Nb5 $8 {now we enter the final stage of the game} 27. Qxd5+ (27. Qc3 $6 Qd1+ 28. Ne1 Rxb4 29. Qc6+ Kb8 30. Rxd1 Rxd1 31. Qe8+ Ka7 $17) 27... Rxd5 28. Rxe4 fxe4 29. axb5 exf3 30. bxa6+ Kxa6 31. gxf3 c5 {I managed to calculate this line when playing 22...-Re4. Despite being pawn down, the activity of my pieces and White's extra pawn being doubled (in addition it's Rook endgame and extra pawn often doesn't mean much here:) ) the position is drawn} 32. Kf1 Kb5 33. Ke2 Re5+ ({I was about to play} 33... Rd4 {but didn't like due to} 34. Rg1 h6 35. Rg4 {Although it's also a draw, Black must be a bit accurate}) 34. Kd3 Rf5 35. Ke3 Re5+ 36. Kd3 Rf5 37. Ke3 h6 (37... Kb4 $5) 38. h4 $1 {the easiest} Re5+ 39. Kd3 Rd5+ 40. Ke3 Re5+ 41. Kd3 Rd5+ 42. Ke3 Re5+ {Conclusion: 1) In the opening I should have played more precise 14...-Qf6! 2) In the middlegame I should have seen the given chance 24...-Na5! But generally speaking, it was quite a smooth game!} 1/2-1/2

