{[%evp 0,28,14,23,23,10,16,16,35,9,17,10,26,19,1,-14,8,19,41,2,-8,-8,-8,-4,3,-13,-9,-22,-22,-28,-32]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Bb7 5. Bg2 Be7 {The classical variation of Queen's Indian Defense. I have used it regularly in my games since 2008!} 6. O-O O-O {Here is the main tabia of QID with ...-Bb7 and ...-Be7. White has a number of different possibilities, my opponent chose} 7. Re1 $5 {This would be one of my main concerns before I found} Ne4 $5 {many players do get surprised seeing this move on the board. It's quite a rare but perhaps the most direct play against the 7.Re1} ({One of the first 'painful' defeats in QID I experienced back in 2009 where I chose another line.} 7... c5 8. d5 exd5 9. Nh4 Ne4 10. cxd5 Bxh4 11. Bxe4 Bf6 12. Nc3 {and I wouldn't even doubt how tricky and dangerous this position might be!} d6 13. Bf4 Qd7 $6 {I played this move with the idea of playing ...-b5 later, but Black's too slow.} 14. Qc2 g6 $4 15. Bg2 {now Black's lost.} Na6 16. Ne4 Be7 17. Bh6 Rfe8 18. Qc3 f6 19. Qxf6 $18 {1-0 (30) Socko,B (2656)-Abasov,N (2505) Warsaw 2009}) 8. Nfd2 {The most critical} d5 ({protectic the e4-Knight with} 8... f5 $6 {is dangerous in a view of} 9. d5 $1 $16) 9. Nxe4 $6 {surprisingly I faced this (inaccurate) move thrice out of 3 times I played 7...-Ne4 against GMs!} ({and never the strongest} 9. cxd5 exd5 10. Nxe4 dxe4 11. Nc3 f5 {where position perhaps is slightly favorable for White but not much.}) 9... dxe4 10. Nc3 f5 11. Be3 Bf6 12. Qd2 (12. f3 {would transpose to my game against Volkov} Nc6 13. fxe4 Bxd4 14. Bxd4 Qxd4+ 15. Qxd4 Nxd4 $36 {and Black's slightly favorable in the endgame. ...½ (31) Volkov,S (2579)-Abasov,N (2531) Konya 2014}) 12... Nc6 13. Rad1 Qe7 {leaving d8-square for my a8-Rook.} 14. f3 Rad8 15. fxe4 Bxd4 16. Bxd4 $2 ({White was already in a danger zone. They had to react accurately in order to maintain the balance in the game.} 16. Qc1 $1 {Only move. I assume my opponent was afraid of} Qc5 17. Bxd4 Rxd4 18. Rxd4 Qxd4+ {after what White loses the c4-pawn, however, with strong} 19. e3 $1 Qxc4 20. exf5 $1 Rxf5 (20... exf5 $4 21. Bd5+ $18) 21. Ne2 $1 {White manages to neutralize Black's initiative!} Qxc1 22. Rxc1 Rc5 23. Rxc5 bxc5 24. Nc3 $11 {and with the next moves White should win the pawn back.}) 16... Nxd4 {Now my King is safer, pieces are more active, as well as I have better pawn structure and space advantage! The domination of Black is unquestionable.} 17. Qc1 fxe4 18. Nxe4 Qf7 {Bringing Queen into the attack!} 19. Qb1 Qh5 ({According to engine} 19... c5 {was even better, however, during the game, it's hard to understand such details.}) 20. Nc3 $1 Bxg2 21. Kxg2 Qe5 22. Qd3 $2 {The move that eased my work with converting the advantage.} Nf5 $1 {I am happy, I managed to assess the occurring 2R vs Q endgame correctly. It's important to consider King's safety, whereas White's King is clearly weaker. Also, my e3-Knight is going to control both d1 and f1-squares, thus e1-Rook gets stuck.} 23. Qxd8 $8 Ne3+ $1 {Important intermezzo} 24. Kg1 Rxd8 25. Rxd8+ Kf7 26. Nd1 {White wants to trade off my powerful Knight} Ke7 {Kicking out the only active piece of White.} (26... Qa5 $5 27. Nc3 Qc5 $19 {was strong too}) 27. Rd3 Nxc4 {White managed to kick out my e3-Knight from its powerful position at the cost of a pawn. Now I have Q+p vs 2R and the activity.} 28. b3 Qc5+ 29. Nf2 Ne5 30. Rd2 Ng4 ({I hesitated to play} 30... Qe3 {in a view of} 31. Red1 Ng4 32. Rd7+ Ke8 33. Rd8+ Kf7 34. R1d7+ Kg6 35. Rf8 {and White seem to be still in the game}) 31. e4 {after provoking 31.e4, the f3-square weakened. Now I return my Knight to e5.} Ne5 $1 32. Kg2 Qc3 33. Ree2 c5 34. Rc2 {Until my opponent made his last move on the board clocks showed zeros, White forfeited on time (in a losing position). I was happy to help my team with the victory with Black pieces against strong GM.} 0-1

[Event "FIDE World Cup"]
