{[%evp 0,77,37,-14,22,22,22,-12,-20,-20,17,-6,33,-19,51,45,34,34,43,28,6,21,19,18,6,8,4,19,30,19,34,27,35,33,26,34,34,11,23,16,13,16,34,19,36,23,22,18,37,14,-31,-34,-21,55,25,67,70,57,128,-3,76,76,82,92,67,67,152,121,226,253,257,257,252,290,327,343,343,343,380,336]} 1. c4 (1. e4 c6 {see commentary on move 5. Qc2 of the game.} (1... e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. Re1 Nd6 6. Nxe5 Be7 7. Bf1 Nxe5 8. Rxe5 O-O 9. Nc3 Ne8 10. Nd5 Bd6 11. Re1 c6 12. Ne3 Be7 13. c4 Nc7 14. d4 d5 15. cxd5 Bb4 16. Bd2 Bxd2 17. Qxd2 Nxd5 18. Nxd5 Qxd5 19. Re5 Qd6 20. Bc4 {See commentary on move 17...-Nxe4 in the game. [#]} Bd7 21. Rae1 Rae8 22. a3 Rxe5 23. Rxe5 Re8 24. Qf4 Be6 25. Bxe6 fxe6 26. Qe4 h6 27. h4 Qe7 28. g3 Qf6 29. Qe3 Re7 30. Kg2 a6 31. Qb3 Kh7 32. Qf3 Qxf3+ 33. Kxf3 Kg6 34. Ke4 Kf6 35. h5 b6 36. b4 Rd7 37. f4 Ra7 38. a4 Rd7 39. g4 Rd8 40. Kd3 Rd7 41. a5 b5 42. Rc5 Rd6 43. Ke4 Ke7 44. Rc1 Kd7 45. Rc5 Ke7 46. g5 Kd7 47. Re5 Ke7 48. g6 Kf6 49. f5 exf5+ 50. Rxf5+ Ke6 51. Rc5 Ke7 52. Re5+ Kf6 53. Rf5+ Ke6 54. Rf7 Rd5 55. Rc7 Kd6 56. Rxg7 Rxh5 57. Ra7 (57. Rg8 Rg5 58. g7 Ke6 59. Re8+ Kf7 60. Ra8 $18) 57... Rg5 58. g7 h5 59. d5 h4 60. Kf4 Rg1 61. dxc6 Kxc6 62. Kf5 Kd5 63. Rxa6 Rxg7 64. Rh6 Rf7+ 65. Kg5 Rg7+ 66. Kf5 Rf7+ 67. Kg5 {1/2-1/2 (67) Heimann,A (2617)-Malakhov,V (2669) Karlsruhe 2020}) 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Bd3 Qc7 {and on} 6. Ne2 {preparing Bf4 Black goes} e5 $5 7. dxe5 Nxe5 8. Bb5+ Bd7 9. Bxd7+ (9. Qxd5 O-O-O $36) 9... Qxd7 {[#] with OK position for Black.}) 1... e6 $5 {To be honest, I wasn't expecting Jaime to play this move. Lately, I played only the Catalan, but I was eager to play something different in this game.} 2. Nc3 d5 3. cxd5 exd5 4. d4 c6 5. Qc2 $5 {Improvisation over the board. I realized the similarity in this position with the Exchange variation in Caro-Kann Defense, hence I decided to play like Black normally does in there.} Bd6 6. Nf3 Ne7 7. e4 $5 {Must and best. Otherwise Black would have developed the Bishop to f5} dxe4 8. Nxe4 Bb4+ 9. Bd2 (9. Nc3 {was an alternative}) 9... Bxd2+ 10. Qxd2 {Now I get the reversed Exchange var. of Caro-Kann with an extra Nf3 on the board. [#]} O-O 11. Bc4 {placing Bishop most actively} Bf5 12. Ng3 Nd7 13. O-O Bg4 14. Ng5 $1 (14. Qf4 $2 {is bad due to} Bxf3 15. Qxf3 Nb6 16. Bb3 Qxd4 $17) 14... Nf6 (14... Nb6 $2 {is just a blunder} 15. Bxf7+ Rxf7 16. Nxf7 Kxf7 17. Qf4+ $18) ({on} 14... h6 {I was planning to play} 15. h3 ({doesn't work} 15. Nxf7 Rxf7 16. Bxf7+ (16. h3 $1) 16... Kxf7 17. Qf4+ {due to} Nf6 $1 {and Black defends on g4})) 15. h3 (15. Rfe1 {could be an alternative} h6 16. N5e4 $36) 15... Bd7 16. Rfe1 h6 {here I spent about 15 minutes deciding how exactly shall I continue the game?} 17. N5e4 {this move looks more active, however, has the the drawback. White has an isolated pawn and should normally avoid the simplifications.} (17. Nf3 $5 {was the alternative but I couldn't understand how I am going to crack the position in case of} Ned5 18. Ne5 Be6 $132) {I was not expecting him to play} 17... Nfd5 $6 {and I believe this move was the first mistake of his. White has activity and isolated pawn, according to rules, Black should have started thinking about simplifying position. As Karpov said, Knight is good on 'd5' (in front of a isolated pawn) but, it does not attack the pawn. So, not always blocking isolated pawn is the best idea.} ({I was mostly worried of} 17... Nf5) ({In case of} 17... Nxe4 18. Rxe4 Nf5 19. Nxf5 Bxf5 20. Re5 {[#] The occured position was reminding me of recently played game Heimann-Malakhov that I mentioned above. Even though the game ended in a draw, the fact that White managed to outplay a very classy player was calming me that, the position is not fully equal and so far it does require certain precision from Black.}) 18. Nc5 {provoking} b6 {with what I have weakened the c6-pawn for a bit.} 19. Nd3 {And continue the journey with the Knight.} Be6 20. Ne5 {The Knight is brought to ideal (for the positions with isolated pawn) e5-square. In addition it eyes the c6-pawn.} Nc7 21. Bd3 {here I spent another 15 minutes on deciding, whether I should bring some support on my Bishop and put pressure on c6-pawn or just retreat the Bishop.} (21. Rac1 Bxc4 22. Rxc4 Ne6 (22... Qd5 23. Nh5 $132) 23. Rxc6 {with a try of complicating matters Black has simple} Qxd4 $11 (23... Nxc6 24. Nxc6 Qg5 25. Qd1 $36)) (21. Qc3 Bxc4 22. Qxc4 Qd5 (22... Ne6 $4 23. Nxf7 $18) 23. Rac1 Qxc4 24. Rxc4 Ne6 $11) 21... f6 ({Whilst calculating the lines after 21.Bd3, I was most expecting Jaime to play} 21... Qd6 {and now if} 22. Ne4 ({then I realized I can keep it simple and play} 22. Rad1 {as} Bxa2 {is too risky for Black} 23. Ne4 Qe6 (23... Qxd4 24. Nc3 $18 {attacking the a2-Bishop and preparing Bh7.}) {and over the board I only considered of} 24. Nc3 $5 ({whilst engine's} 24. Ng4 $1 $18 {wins the game on the spot})) {Black can take on d4} 22... Qxd4 {and the line I calculated initially} 23. Nf6+ Kh8 24. Re4 Qd6 25. Rh4 Nf5 26. Qf4 Qe7 {wasn't giving White much}) 22. Nf3 (22. Ng4 $144 $5) 22... Bf7 23. Nh4 $1 {Knight is heading to exploit the weakened light squares!} Re8 24. Qf4 $1 {The Queen is brought closer to rivalry King!} Ne6 25. Qe4 $1 ({the tempting} 25. Rxe6 {doesn't win the game due to} Bxe6 26. Qe4 Qd5 {and White's attack has no follow up.} 27. Qh7+ Kf8 $44) ({problem with the immediate} 25. Qg4 {was} Qxd4 $1 {and White has got no much here}) 25... Ng5 {distracting the Knight from the d4-pawn.} ({if} 25... Qxd4 {White wins by force with numerous lines. For instance,} 26. Qh7+ Kf8 27. Qh8+ Bg8 (27... Ng8 28. Ng6+ Bxg6 29. Bxg6 $18) 28. Rxe6 Qxd3 29. Rxf6+ gxf6 30. Qxf6+ Bf7 31. Ngf5 $1 $18) (25... Nxd4 26. Qh7+ Kf8 27. Qh8+ Ng8 28. Ngf5) (25... Nf8 26. Qg4 $18 {[%cal Gh4f5]}) 26. Qg4 g6 $2 {Black was planning to play ...-Kg7 next and consolidate, however, the text move was a serious blunder} (26... Nd5 {would be a try to release the tension}) {I didn't calculate any line and immediately went for} 27. Rxe7 $1 Rxe7 28. Nxg6 {after the exchange sacrifice, Black King became extremely vulnerable as well as the -f5 & -h5 squares became available for my g3-Knight.} ({also reasonable was} 28. Bxg6 $5) 28... Rd7 29. Ne2 $6 {Not a bad move in general, but according to engine misses the direct win.} (29. Qh5 {was strong, the line I couldn't realize how much I'm winning was:} Qe8 30. Qxh6 Bxg6 31. Bxg6 Qf8 {and here, after simple} 32. Qh5 {followed by Nf5, Black is defenseless}) ({another option was} 29. Re1 {but it's much more complicated!} Rxd4 30. Qh5 Rxd3 31. Qxh6 Bxg6 32. Qxg6+ Kh8 33. Nh5 Nf3+ (33... Nxh3+ 34. Kf1 $8 $18) 34. Kh1 Qe7 35. Qh6+ Kg8 36. Nxf6+ Qxf6 37. Qxf6 Nxe1 38. Qe6+ Kf8 39. Qxe1 $18) 29... Be6 $2 (29... Bxg6 30. Bxg6 Rg7 {would be the best defense for Black.}) 30. Qg3 {keeping the Quene on the same file with opponent's King.} ({and again} 30. Qh5 {was a better continuation according to engine.}) 30... Rg7 31. h4 Bf7 {it took me a while to understand} 32. Nef4 {is strong} a5 {bringing a8-Rook to defense via the seventh rank.} ({initially I wasn't sure how to play after} 32... Qxd4 33. hxg5 (33. Ne7+ Kf8 34. Nf5 $2 {but there's a serious issue} Qxf4 $1 $17 {and Black has serious initiative}) 33... hxg5 34. Ne7+ Kf8 {only after some why I noticed} 35. Nfg6+ $1 $18 (35. Nf5 $2 Qxf4 $17)) 33. Re1 {the last piece joins the attack.} Ra7 34. hxg5 fxg5 35. Ne6 {now White's material advantage, more active pieces and the attack. Position is absolutely winning.} Qe8 36. Ngf8 $1 {it was nice to place the Knight between the Queen and King on f8} Re7 37. Re3 Bxe6 38. Nxe6 {eleventh move with the g1-Knight!} Rgf7 39. Bc4 Kh7 40. Nc7 {Iconic, to finish the game with the Sir Lancelot!} 1-0

