{[%evp 0,51,19,31,34,25,53,26,40,25,37,10,17,-28,-10,-23,-18,-9,11,11,41,47,46,46,56,49,64,45,51,32,81,83,81,79,88,88,110,89,282,279,244,256,264,273,388,383,384,384,384,392,402,380,472,481]} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. c4 $5 {Not that easy to face it without knowledge} Nc6 6. d3 {a new move for my opponent} ({After the game he said he remembered the game of Mamedyarov} 6. Nc3 Nxc3 7. dxc3 g6 8. Bg5 Be7 9. Bh6 Bf8 10. Bg5 Be7 11. Bh6 Bf8 12. Bg5 {½, Jones,G (2640)-Mamedyarov,S (2804) Wijk aan Zee 2018. In fact, I was the one seconding Mamedyarov in that tournament and that's when I realized 6.d3 is a tricky move!}) 6... Nf6 7. d4 d5 8. c5 $5 {[%cal Gf1b5] is the main idea of the concept} Bg4 $6 ({Usually they go} 8... Be7) 9. Bb5 Bxf3 $6 {I believe he shouldn't have reacted that way, but he was perhaps worried about my Knight eventually hoping on e5.} (9... Be7 10. Bxc6+ bxc6 11. Qa4 {[%cal Gf3e5]} Bxf3 12. Qxc6+ Kf8 13. gxf3 $14) 10. Qxf3 Be7 11. Nc3 O-O 12. Be3 $14 {now White has a Bishop pair and spatial advantage} Nb4 13. Qd1 (13. Qe2 {perhaps would be even better. I wanted to keep e2 square free for the Bishop} c6 14. a3 $16) 13... c6 14. Be2 ({To be honest, initially I was planning} 14. a3 $5 {then I saw} a5 $1 {and wan't sure how to react after} (14... cxb5 15. axb4 $16 {looked too good to me}) {if} 15. O-O Na6 {[%cal Ga6c7,Gc7e6] Knight redeploys to e6} (15... cxb5 16. axb4 axb4 17. Nxb5 $16) {yes, White perhaps is still better after} 16. Bd3 {but that wasn't enough for me}) 14... b5 $2 {I was super surprised seeing this on board} ({I was expecting him to play} 14... a5 {yet trying to bring Knight to e6, via a6-c7.} 15. O-O Na6 16. Qb3 {was what I was considering playing}) (14... Na6 {would still be better than the text move} 15. Bxa6 bxa6 16. O-O) 15. a3 Na6 16. a4 {now Black's position collapses} b4 17. Na2 ({After} 17. Bxa6 {I didn't continue to calculate seeing} Qa5 (17... bxc3 18. b4)) 17... Qa5 18. O-O Rab8 $2 {most natural but loses immediately} 19. Nc1 {[%cal Gc1b3] Knight and Queen in trouble. You can save one.} b3 (19... Nc7 20. Nb3 $18 {traps the Queen}) 20. Bd2 (20. Nxb3 Qb4 {gives Black some hopes}) 20... Nb4 21. Qe1 $1 Rfc8 22. Nxb3 Qc7 23. Bxb4 Qb7 24. Bd1 Qxb4 25. Qxe7 Re8 26. Qxa7 1-0

