{[%evp 0,35,27,45,18,17,17,-18,-17,-17,-26,-26,7,-21,-35,-29,-6,-13,-10,-11,-3,10,8,9,10,26,14,19,12,12,-2,-3,-3,21,25,24,16,0] After a painful loss in round 5, I needed to cool down and to my luck, it was a rest day. This day I took a day off of chess, went to the city center - had some sightseeing, and had a good sleep. I only started preparing for round 6 only half an hour before the round. The only thing I had on my mind was - my first move is going to be} 1. e4 $5 {I hadn't played the first move with the King's pawn for already half a year.} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. c3 $5 {Ponziani. Risky decision, however, considering the fact that Andrey started playing 1...-e5 relatively recently, I hoped he never took into serious account the opening. Also, since the game was after the rest day, I believed he spent quite a lot of time on preparing against 1. d4.} Nf6 (3... d5 4. Qa4 $5) 4. d4 exd4 ({and of course the most critical is} 4... Nxe4 5. d5 Bc5 $1 {position gets double-edged. Without memorizing all critical lines it's hard to enter this variation} (5... Ne7 6. Nxe5 {looks pleasant for White}) 6. dxc6 Bxf2+ 7. Ke2 Bb6 8. Qd5 Nf2 9. Rg1 O-O 10. cxb7 Bxb7 11. Qxb7 Qf6 12. Qd5 c6 $44) 5. e5 Nd5 6. Bc4 {following Hou-Carlsen game} ({alternatives are} 6. cxd4) ({or} 6. Bb5 $5) 6... Nb6 7. Bb3 Be7 {out of my book} ({in case of} 7... dxc3 8. Nxc3 {White has compensation for a pawn thanks to better development}) (7... d5 {was played by Magnus} 8. cxd4 Bg4 9. Be3 f6 10. O-O (10. h3 Bh5 11. Nc3 $132 {would be my play}) 10... Qd7 11. h3 Bh5 12. e6 Qd6 13. Nc3 O-O-O 14. a4 a5 15. Nb5 Qxe6 16. Bf4 Rd7 17. Re1 Qf7 18. Rc1 Bb4 {0-1 (66) Hou,Y (2603)-Carlsen,M (2861) Wijk aan Zee 2013}) 8. O-O {Spent 13 minutes} (8. cxd4 d6) 8... d6 (8... dxc3 {was perhaps possible here} 9. Nxc3 O-O 10. Bf4 d6 {and Black looks solid}) 9. exd6 ({Position arising after} 9. cxd4 dxe5 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. dxe5 Qxd1 12. Rxd1 Be6 {Looked very comfy for Black} (12... a5 $5) 13. Bxe6 fxe6 14. Nc3 Kf7 $11) 9... Qxd6 10. Nxd4 $5 (10. cxd4 Bg4 $132 {followed by castling queenside}) 10... Nxd4 11. cxd4 c6 12. Nc3 O-O 13. Re1 {Despite the fact that I had isolated pawn, I was happy about the outcome of the opening.} Bf6 {Here I spent quite some time calculating straightforward lines} 14. Be3 ({such as} 14. Ne4 $5 Qxd4 15. Qh5 (15. Qf3 $5 {looked really interesting which I kind of overlooked} Be6 (15... Nd5 $2 16. Rd1 Qe5 17. Bxd5 cxd5 18. Rxd5 $1 $18) 16. Bh6 Be5 17. Rad1 Qb4 18. Qh5 $40 {White has strong domination}) 15... Nd5 16. Bd2 {bringing more pieces into the game} Qxb2 17. Bxd5 cxd5 18. Nxf6+ Qxf6 19. Bb4 Be6 20. Bxf8 Rxf8 $44 {didn't seem appealing for White}) (14. Qh5 Be6 15. Ne4 Qxd4 16. Bg5 Bxg5 17. Nxg5 h6 18. Nxe6 fxe6 19. Bxe6+ Kh8 $11 {and Black should be ok}) 14... Bf5 (14... Be6 $5 {is what I was most concerned about} 15. Ne4 (15. Bc2 Rad8 16. Ne4 Qe7 17. Qd3 g6 18. Nxf6+ Qxf6 $11 {and White has not much}) 15... Bxb3 $1 (15... Qe7 $2 16. d5 $18) 16. Nxd6 (16. Qxb3 Qd8 $11) 16... Bxd1 17. Raxd1 Nd5 18. Nxb7 Rfb8 19. Na5 Rxb2 20. Nxc6 Rxa2 21. Ra1 Rxa1 22. Rxa1 Re8 {leads to a draw}) 15. Qf3 Qd7 16. h3 {covering g4-square and preparing centralization of the a1-Rook} Rad8 17. Rad1 Nd5 18. Bxd5 $1 {I like this move. Yes, it might be not the top choice of the engine, however, it predetermines the pawn structure for the remaining part of the game.} cxd5 19. Bf4 {By giving up Bishop pair I wanted to stabilyze position in the center, next, my goal was to trade of dark squared Bishops and get better Knight vs worse Bishop (due to d5-pawn being on the same color as the Bishop) just like in the game Saidy-Fischer!} Bc2 (19... Bg6 20. Be5 Bg5 21. Qg3 (21. Bf4 $11) 21... f6 (21... Bh6 22. Bc7 Rde8 23. Bd6 $16) 22. Bf4 (22. Bc7 Rde8) 22... Bxf4 23. Qxf4 $36 {[%csl Gc3,Rg6]}) 20. Rd2 Bg6 21. Be5 Bg5 (21... Be7) 22. Rde2 Qf5 $6 {not a good move according to engine, however, practically - position was already not that easy, since White was threatening h4!} ({e.g.} 22... b5 23. h4 $1 Bxh4 24. Qf4 {and Black cannot defend the Bishop properly} b4 25. Nd1 f6 26. Qxh4 fxe5 27. dxe5 {[%cal Ge5e6,Ge6e7]} Qe6 28. Qd4 $16) (22... Rfe8 23. Bc7 Rxe2 24. Qxe2 Rc8 25. Qg4 Qxg4 26. hxg4 Bd2 27. Nxd5 {looked good for White during the game}) 23. Qg3 {now I'm threatening to play Bd6.} f6 (23... Bh5 24. Bxg7 $1 Bf4 (24... Kxg7 25. Re5 $18) 25. Qh4 Kxg7 26. Re5 Bxe5 27. Rxe5 $16 {White either attacks the rivalry King or win d5-pawn}) (23... Rfe8 $2 24. h4 Bh6 25. Bc7 Rxe2 26. Rxe2 Rc8 27. Qd6 $18) 24. Bd6 Rfe8 25. Rxe8+ Rxe8 26. Rxe8+ Bxe8 27. f4 $1 {restricting opponent's pieces} Bh6 28. Qe3 Bc6 (28... Bf7 29. Qe7 Qc8 30. Kh2 $16) 29. Qe7 Qc8 (29... g6 {was perhaps a better continuation, trying to keep the dark-squared Bishop in the game.}) (29... g5 $2 30. g4 Qg6 (30... Qd3 31. fxg5 $1) 31. Qd8+ Qe8 (31... Kf7 32. f5 $18) 32. Qxe8+ Bxe8 33. Nxd5 Bg7 34. f5 $18) 30. Kh2 $1 {important move against ...-g6!} ({in the line} 30. g4 $6 g6 31. Qxf6 $2 Bg7 $11 {White King is misplaced on g1!}) 30... b5 31. b4 $5 {keeps position unchanged} (31. Qxa7 {was totally possible} b4 {and position after} 32. Nd1 $18 {is absolutely winning, however, I didn't want to give any kind counterplay}) 31... a6 32. a3 g6 (32... Kh8 33. g4 {followed by g5, trapping the Bishop}) 33. Qxf6 Bg7 34. Qg5 {and now} Bxd4 {is not coming with the check! That was the whole point of 30.Kh2!} 35. Nxd5 Qd7 (35... Qe6 36. Qd8+ (36. Qe7 Qf7) 36... Qe8 37. Ne7+ Kf7 38. Nxc6 Qxc6 39. Qe7+ Kg8 40. Qf8#) 36. Qe7 Qe8 (36... h5 $1 {strong move what we both missed} 37. Be5 (37. Nf6+ Bxf6 38. Qf8+ Kh7 39. Qxf6 Qg7 40. Qg5) 37... Bxe5 38. Qxd7 Bxd7 39. fxe5 Kf7 40. Nf4 h4 41. Nd3 {and White should still be winning}) ({Initially he was planning to play} 36... Bg7 37. Nf6+ Bxf6 38. Qf8#) 37. Be5 Bxe5 38. fxe5 Qf7 39. e6 {I was really happy about managing to beat a player of Andrey's caliber in the manner of classical players!} 1-0

[Event "FIDE World Cup"]
[Site "Khanty-Mansiysk"]
[Date "2019.09.14"]
