1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 {I played 1.e4-c5 2.Nf3-Nc6 6 times in the World Cup 2019 and in all 6 games I faced one and only - Rossolimo.} ({Nowadays you rarely see} 3. d4 {being played!}) 3... g6 4. Bxc6 dxc6 5. d3 Bg7 6. h3 Nf6 7. Nc3 ({Just for those who never close paid attention} 7. Be3 $2 {move order is bad due to} Nxe4 $1 8. dxe4 Qxd1+ 9. Kxd1 Bxb2 10. c3 Bxa1 11. Kc2 b6 $17) 7... Nd7 8. Be3 e5 9. Qd2 h6 10. O-O b6 11. Nh2 ({alternative is} 11. a4) 11... Nf6 $5 $146 {Weird looking novelty. But what's the idea of playing 7...-Nd7 and then simply returning with the Knight back to -f6? Elementary, my dear Watson! We fight against the f4 move!} ({Clearly the logical follow up (in 7...-Nd7 variation) is to bring the Knight to -d4 via -f8 and -e6.} 11... Nf8 12. f4 exf4 13. Bxf4 Ne6 {and the big theory only begins here!}) 12. a3 {Was played after 15 minutes of thinking.} (12. f4 {now is leading to double-edged positions} exf4 13. Bxf4 g5 ({or} 13... Nh5 14. Be3 Be6 $132) 14. Be3 Be6 {followed by ...-Qc7 and castling long side!}) {White's idea is obvious - they are preparing the thematic breakthrough on the queenside with b4. What shall Black do in this case?} 12... Be6 {play in the center!} (12... a5 {doesn't help black much as after} 13. Na4 {b4 is even more powerful, since the b6-pawn was weakened}) 13. b4 Nd5 $1 {Came as a shocker for my opponent!} (13... c4) 14. exd5 {was played after half an hour (!) of thinking.} (14. bxc5 Nxe3 15. Qxe3 Qd4 16. cxb6 {is an interesting try} (16. Qxd4 exd4 17. Ne2 bxc5 18. Rfb1 O-O-O) 16... Qxc3 17. b7 Rb8 18. Qxa7 O-O 19. a4 {now White is marching the a-pawn! It looks scary for Black, isn't it? Luckily I have defensive resource by bringing the Bishop to -c5} Rfd8 20. a5 Bf8 21. Qb6 (21. a6 $4 Bc5 $19 {traps the Queen}) 21... Bc5 22. Qb1 (22. Qxc6 $4 Bxf2+ $19) (22. Qc7 Bd6 23. Qb6 Bc5 $11) 22... Ba7 $132 {and I'm in time to block the pawns}) 14... cxd5 {now, it turns out that White cannot save a piece. If Knight retreats, ...-d4 traps the Bishop.} 15. Qe1 (15. bxc5 d4 16. cxb6 dxe3 17. Qxe3 Qxb6 18. Qxb6 axb6 $44 {and Black's Bishops compensate the absence of a pawn.}) 15... d4 16. Bd2 dxc3 17. Bxc3 f6 (17... O-O 18. Bxe5 Bxh3 19. Bxg7 Qg5 20. Qe4 Kxg7 21. f4 Qg3 22. bxc5 bxc5 23. Rf2 Rfe8 24. Qc4 {and Black has difficulties here}) (17... Qc7 {was a better option according to the engine.}) 18. f4 O-O 19. fxe5 f5 {According to the engine - this position is better for White. Yet, this is the exact type of the position I was aiming for. The reason is - I was too much inspired by AlphaZero's way of playing - sacrificing material, closing the center and playing for a long-term compensation. With my Bishop on -e6, it is not clear how White can open up the position and try using the extra material. Meanwhile, Black's play is much simpler. They will eventually push the kingside pawns!} 20. bxc5 bxc5 21. Nf3 Qc7 22. Qf2 Qc6 23. a4 Bd5 24. Nd2 Rac8 25. Rfe1 g5 26. Ra3 Qe6 {blocking the e-pawn.} ({in case of} 26... f4 {White would have played} 27. e6 $1 {changing the type of the position}) 27. Raa1 Qc6 {starting to repeat the moves} ({Here, I should have gone for} 27... h5 {I'm not even certain why I didn't play it!}) 28. a5 h5 29. Ra3 Qe6 30. Ra4 Qc6 ({In case of} 30... Bh6 {followed by ...-g4 and taking the initiative on the kingside, I was concerned about} 31. h4 $5 {and if} g4 {White plays} 32. Rf4 $5 $132 {[%cal Gd2f1,Gf1g3] yes, I can win an exchange, but what then? Whilst White can strengthen the position with bringing the Knight to -g3.}) 31. Ra3 Qe6 32. Ra4 Qc6 33. Ra3 {despite the fact that the game lasted only 33 moves and it ended in a draw, I really enjoyed playing it!} 1/2-1/2

