1. Nf3 d5 2. d4 Nf6 3. c4 e6 4. Nc3 Bb4 {Ragozin. Frankly speaking, I wasn't expecting Ragozin from Sanan.} 5. Bg5 $5 ({Some rounds earlier in this tournament I had faced Ragozin in my game against Anand where I chose} 5. Qa4+) 5... h6 6. Bxf6 Qxf6 7. e3 O-O 8. Rc1 dxc4 9. Bxc4 c5 10. dxc5 Bxc5 11. Qc2 {I already had couple games here, surprisingly Sanan wasn't ready facing it again!} Be7 $146 {Novelty. I was totally surprised seeing it on the board.} ({I even had a game here playing with Black, then I chose} 11... Nc6 12. Ne4 Qe7 (12... Bb4+ 13. Ke2 Qf5 14. g4 $5 {looks like a serious try with White}) 13. Nxc5 Qxc5 14. a3 Qa5+ 15. Qc3 Qxc3+ 16. Rxc3 Rd8 17. Ke2 Bd7 18. Rhc1 Rac8 19. Bd3 Kf8 20. h4 Ne7 {and eventually game ended in a draw: 1/2 (42)-1/2 (42) Salem,A (2586)-Abasov,N (2509) Sharjah 2014}) 12. O-O (12. Bd3 Nc6 (12... a6 13. O-O Nc6 14. Bh7+ Kh8 15. Ne4) 13. a3 Ne5) 12... Nc6 13. Rfd1 Rd8 14. Rxd8+ ({or} 14. Ne4 Qf5 15. Rxd8+ Nxd8 16. Nd4 Qa5 17. Bd3 Bd7 18. Qc7 Qd5 19. Bc4 Qxe4 20. Qxd7 Bf8 21. Bf1 $36) 14... Bxd8 15. Rd1 {Thanks to the activity of my pieces and extra half an hour on the clocks, I was very optimistic about my chances.} Qe7 16. Qd3 ({in case of} 16. Qd2 Ba5 {comes with the pin}) 16... Ba5 $6 ({Black should have tried to finish the development with} 16... b6 17. Nb5 Bb7 18. Nd6 Nb4 $132 {followed by 19...-Bxf3}) 17. Bb5 Qb4 $2 {Black is not really threatening taking on b2} 18. Ne4 $1 Bc7 (18... Qxb2 $4 19. Rb1 Qxa2 20. Bc4 Qa4 21. Nc5 $18 {traps the Queen}) 19. a3 Qe7 (19... Qxb2 20. Rb1 Qa2 21. Bc4 $18) 20. Nd6 Rb8 (20... Qxd6 {leads to losing endgame} 21. Qxd6 Bxd6 22. Rxd6 Kf8 23. Bxc6 Ke7 24. Rd4 bxc6 25. Ne5 c5 26. Nc6+ Kf6 27. Rf4+ $18) 21. Bxc6 ({in case of a waiting move, I wasn't happy about} 21. h3 Qxd6 22. Qxd6 Bxd6 23. Rxd6 Kf8 24. Bxc6 Ke7 25. Rd4 bxc6 26. Ne5 {and this time Black's got} Rb6) (21. b4 a5 {was my concern}) 21... bxc6 22. Ne5 (22. Nxc8 Rxc8 23. Qa6 {doesn't lead to a material win in a view of} Rd8 $1 {and Black is in the game}) 22... Qxd6 $2 ({Here we both missed a very powerful resource for Black} 22... Bd7 $1 {is looks totally insane to give away the f7-pawn. But... If it works - it works...} 23. Ndxf7 (23. Nexf7 Be8) 23... Be8 24. Nd6 Rd8 25. Nec4 c5 $1 {[%cal Ge8b5] preparing ...-Bb5} 26. e4 Bb5 27. e5 Bxc4 28. Qxc4 Bxd6 29. exd6 Rxd6 {and at the end of this extraordinary line, somehow, Black holds!}) 23. Qxd6 Bxd6 24. Rxd6 Rxb2 25. h4 {opening some air for my King, at the same time will try to get rivalry King into a cage with h5 later on. The endgame is extremely unpleasant for Black} Bb7 26. Rd8+ Kh7 27. Nxf7 (27. h5 $6 {is the alternative I was considering, but it fails to work due to} Rb1+ 28. Kh2 Rb5 29. f4 f6 $132) 27... Kg6 28. Ne5+ (28. Rf8 $5 $18) (28. Rd7 c5 29. Ne5+ Kf6 30. Ng4+ Kf5 31. f3 $18) 28... Kf6 29. f4 Kf5 {Here I panicked for a bit as the Black King gets suddenly active...} 30. Nd3 $2 (30. Nc4 $1 {with the idea of Nd6 was the key move} Rb1+ 31. Kh2 c5 32. Nd6+ Kg6 33. Rb8 Be4 34. h5+ $1 Kh7 (34... Kxh5 35. Rg8 $18) 35. Rc8 $18) 30... Rb5 (30... Rb1+ 31. Kf2 $1 (31. Kh2 {could lead to crazy complications!} Ke4 32. Nc5+ Kxe3 33. Rb8 {seems like White's won?} (33. a4 {is engine's suggestion} Kxf4 34. Nxe6+ Ke3 35. Nc5 Rb4 36. Rb8 Rxh4+ 37. Kg3 Rb4 38. a5 Rb5 39. Nxb7 c5 40. Re8+ Kd3 41. Rd8+ Ke2 42. Nd6 Rxa5 43. Nc4 {Practically White still has chances but with the precise play the game should end in a draw.}) 33... Rb5 $1 {No!} 34. Nxb7 Kf2 $1 {Now White's totally tied up} (34... c5 $144 {should also draw} 35. f5 $2 e5 $1 $17) 35. a4 (35. Kh3 h5 $11) 35... Rb1 36. a5 a6 $11 ({or} 36... Rg1 $11)) 31... Ba6 32. Nc5 Bc4 33. Kg3 {[%cal Ge3e4] White's domination is unquestionble}) 31. Nf2 $2 {too passive, but the time trouble approaching (I had 7 minutes vs 2 minutes) I was a bit concerned about ...-Ke4 or ...-Kg4 moves.} e5 (31... c5 32. h5 {[%cal Gd8f8] threatening Rf8 checkmate} e5 33. Rf8+ Ke6 34. Re8+ Kd7 35. Rxe5 {looked better for White at first glance, but} Rb1+ 36. Kh2 c4 37. Rc5 Rb2 $1 {Black has enough counter-play} (37... Rc1 $2 38. Nd3 $1 Rc2 $4 (38... Kd6 39. Nxc1 Kxc5 40. Na2 {[%cal Ga2c3] Bringing Knight to -c3, just like Nimzowitsch recommended!} c3 $1 41. Nxc3 Kc4 42. Ne2 Kb3 43. g4 Kxa3 44. g5 Bd5 $1 (44... Bf3 $2 45. Ng3 Kb4 46. Nf5 a5 47. Nxg7 a4 48. gxh6 Be4 49. f5 a3 50. h7 a2 51. h8=Q $18 {and White is promoting first, with 3 extra pawns}) 45. Ng3 (45. Nc3 Bf3 $1 46. f5 hxg5 47. f6 Bxh5 48. fxg7 Bf7 49. Nb5+ Kb4 50. Nxa7 Kc5 $11) 45... Kb4 46. e4 (46. Nf5 hxg5 47. fxg5 a5 48. h6 (48. Nxg7 a4 49. h6 Be4 $19) 48... gxh6 49. gxh6 Bg8 50. Ne7 Bh7 51. Nc6+ Kb5 52. Nxa5 Kxa5 $11 {and Black saves the game}) 46... Bg8 $8 47. e5 Kc5 48. Nf5 hxg5 49. e6 $5 {last attempt} Bxe6 50. Nxg7 Bg8 51. fxg5 Kd5 52. Kg3 a5 53. g6 a4 54. h6 a3 55. h7 Bxh7 56. gxh7 a2 57. h8=Q a1=Q {and this endgame is theoretically drawn}) 39. Rxc4 $18) 38. Kg3 Rc2) 32. g4+ {pushing King back} Ke6 33. f5+ Ke7 34. Rg8 $1 {forcing King to f7 from where it might potentially be forked with Nd6.} Kf7 35. Rb8 c5 36. Nd3 (36. Ne4 {would have been a very strong move if only not...} Rb1+ 37. Kf2 Bxe4 $19 {and the Rook on b1 is defended!}) 36... Kf6 $4 {blunders the next move of White} ({meanwhile} 36... c4 $1 {was saving the game} 37. Nxe5+ Rxe5 38. Rxb7+ Kf6 39. Kf2 Rc5 (39... h5 $11 40. g5+ Kxf5 41. Rxg7 Kg4 $11) 40. Ke2 c3 41. Kd1 Rc4 42. Rb4 Rxb4 43. axb4 h5 44. gxh5 Kxf5 45. Kc2 Kg4 46. Kxc3 Kxh4 47. Kd4 Kxh5 48. Kd5 g5 49. e4 Kg6 50. Kd6 Kf7 51. Kd7 Kf6 52. Kd6 Kf7 $11) {I spent 5 out of 6 remanining minutes and only the very last moment spotted} 37. Re8 $1 {now it's totally winning for White} (37. Nxc5 Rxc5 38. Rxb7 Rc4 (38... h5) 39. Rb4) 37... Bd5 {preventing Re6} 38. Nxe5 Rb7 39. e4 $1 Bf7 (39... Bb3 40. Ng6 {followed by e5 and Rf8.}) 40. Nd7+ {Sadly, Sanan resigned here, not letting me deliver the checkmate on the board} (40. Nd7+ Rxd7 41. e5# {would have been a really beautiful finish - checkmate with the contribution of 4 pawns!}) (40. g5+ {was also an option} hxg5 41. Ng4#) 1-0

